@page "/"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.FileSystem
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.Sync
@using Backlog.Modules.Inbox.Abstractions.DataTransferObjects
@inject TasksDesktopState State
@inject InboxDesktopState InboxState
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@inject IToastChannel Toasts
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IAppFeatureSettings Features
@inject IAzureFoundryChatClient AzureFoundryChat
@inject DevbookScope Devbook
@inject ShellNavigationStore ShellNavigation
@* Injected hard rather than resolved: the Inbox's Capture button is on screen
   whenever the Inbox is, and a host that composes the Inbox without the module
   behind it should fail at startup rather than quietly offer a dead button. *@
@inject ICaptureRunner CaptureRunner

@*
    This page. It owns the header and the AI panel, and decides which surface sits
    below them.

    It used to be called the shell, and it owned the update and feedback windows
    too. Those were never about this page — MainLayout mounts them below every
    route now, in AppFooter — and what is left here is genuinely this screen's:
    the repository scope, the view switcher, the pane strip, and the surfaces they
    choose between. The `.shell` class on the wrapper is the older name and stays,
    because it is what every layout rule in app.css is written against.

    There are three, and exactly one of them renders: the workspace, which is the
    roadmap band above the global panes; the tools surface; and the dashboard,
    which holds the session list as its second tab. The last two are takeovers —
    Dev PC Management and the Dashboard are whole domains, and opening one means
    the reader has stopped looking at the backlog, so it takes the screen and hides
    every other domain concern rather than docking beside them. Whichever branch
    renders owns the page's single <main> landmark.

    The header follows the surface. Every control in it acts on something, and a
    control whose something is off screen is a dead button: the roadmap toggle
    and the panes strip configure the workspace, the GitHub check refreshes the
    task list, and Ask AI
    answers from the entries in view — so each of them renders only while what it
    acts on does. The surface switcher and the identity region stay, because they
    are true of the screen whatever is on it.

    What is inside a surface is not the shell's business: Inbox, Backlog
    Management, Devbook, Roadmap, Dev PC Management, Sessions and the
    Dashboard each render themselves from their own project, and the only thing
    that crosses between them here is the repository scope handed to the knowledge
    pane and the dashboard, and the triaged item handed back from the inbox.
*@

<div class="shell shell--wide">
    @* The header is four regions rather than one row of controls: who you are and
       over which repositories, what you are looking at, how the workspace is
       doing, and the things that cut across all of it.

       They used to be one flat strip of near-identical pills, which made a
       multi-select, an exclusive view switch, a panel disclosure and two modal
       utilities look like the same kind of control. Grouping them is the whole
       change — almost every behaviour below is the behaviour that was already
       here. *@
    <header class="app-header">
        @* Region A. It used to open with a "Backlog" wordmark, set smaller than the
           other screens' `.app-header h1` to say it was chrome rather than a page
           title. The window border says the name already, so the wordmark was the
           app telling you twice which app you were in and spending the width of the
           word to do it — the region now opens on the repository scope, which is
           what the identity region was actually for. *@
        <div class="app-header__identity">
            @if (State.Repositories.Count > 0)
            {
                @* One repository on a plain press, one more with Ctrl held — while the
                   task list or the dashboard is on screen; see ScopeRepository below.
                   The anchor — the first one taken, the one Devbook reads — is marked only
                   once a second chip is pressed: with one chip there is nothing for it
                   to be distinct from, and the markup stays exactly what the single
                   select produced.

                   Fused, because several can be on at once. The header draws its groups
                   in two shapes and the shape is the cardinality: members touching
                   inside one border where any number can be pressed, standalone
                   controls where exactly one can — see Region B. The scope used to be
                   loose chips, which said "one of these" to a reader who could in fact
                   take several. The shared ButtonGroup wears the same header-group
                   shape the panes strip does; the chips inside keep their chip
                   classes, because the active fill, the identity edge and the anchor
                   mark all key on those. *@
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group app-header__repository-scope" AriaLabel="Repository scope">
                    @foreach (var repository in State.Repositories)
                    {
                        var selected = State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Contains(repository.Alias, StringComparer.Ordinal);
                        var anchor = selected && State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count > 1
                            && string.Equals(State.AnchorRepositoryAlias, repository.Alias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip chip--scope"
                                      CssClass="@RepositoryScopeChipClass(repository.Alias, anchor)"
                                      PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                      Pressed="selected"
                                      TestId="repository-filter-option"
                                      Title="@RepositoryScopeChipTitle(repository, selected, anchor)"
                                      aria-current="@(anchor ? "true" : null)"
                                      OnClick="args => ScopeRepository(repository.Alias, args)">
                            @repository.Alias
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>

                @* Whether the identity hues are drawn at all — here, beside the chips
                   that wear the most visible ones, rather than buried in Settings with
                   the choice of which hue each repository gets. Those are two different
                   questions asked by two different people at two different moments: one
                   is "this project is green", set once and left alone, and this one is
                   "not right now", which is a decision about the screen in front of you
                   and belongs on it.

                   Outside the scope group on purpose. Inside it this would read as a
                   sixth repository, and it is not a scope — using it narrows nothing.

                   A switch rather than a chip, and the difference is what it is about.
                   Every other control in this strip answers "which of these am I looking
                   at" and holds a selection; this one answers "is this layer on", which
                   is the one question a switch is for. So the shared Toggle in its
                   default shape: role="switch" and aria-checked on a real button, with
                   the name written beside the track instead of on it — which is also why
                   there is no aria-label here. Toggle labels itself from that text
                   through aria-labelledby, and a second accessible name would displace
                   the one the reader can actually see.

                   It renders with the chips or not at all. With nothing configured there
                   is no repository to be an identity for, and a control governing the
                   look of an empty set is a control with nothing to do — the same reason
                   the surface switcher below hides itself when there is only one
                   surface. *@
                <Toggle CssClass="header-switch"
                        LabelCssClass="header-switch__label"
                        ControlCssClass="toggle__track header-switch__track"
                        Label="Colors"
                        Id="repository-colours-switch"
                        TestId="repository-colours-toggle"
                        Checked="State.RepositoryColoursVisible"
                        CheckedChanged="ShowRepositoryColours" />
            }
        </div>

        @* Region B — navigation, and nothing else. Two groups and one toggle,
           because there are three different questions: which surface owns the
           screen (exactly one), whether the roadmap band is up (on or off), and
           which panes are on it (any number). Two shared ButtonGroups and a lone
           ToggleButton make those differences visible; one undifferentiated row of
           pills could not.

           And they are drawn differently, because the count is the difference.
           Members fused inside one border say "any of these, together": that is the
           panes strip, and the repository scope in Region A. Standalone controls
           with a gap between them say "one of these", or "this one, on or off":
           that is the surface switcher, and the roadmap toggle. The surface
           switcher and the roadmap used to wear the fused shape on the argument
           that everything here answers "what am I looking at" — but a reader
           cannot see how many answers a control takes until they press it, and the
           shape is the one thing they can see first. *@
        <nav class="app-header__nav" aria-label="Workspace views">
            @* One field with three states, in markup that finally says so.
               WorkspaceSurface has always been a single enum precisely because a
               takeover cannot coexist with the workspace or with another
               takeover — but the header rendered independent buttons carrying
               aria-expanded, which described neither the exclusivity nor the fact
               that a takeover replaces the workspace instead of expanding beside
               it. A group of pressed states describes both, and it gives the way
               back a control of its own rather than leaving it to a second press or
               the in-surface close button.

               Loose rather than fused: exactly one surface is on at a time, and a
               fused strip is the shape this header reserves for "several at once".
               The group is still one group — one role, one label, the options in
               one tab sequence — it only stops drawing its members as one control.

               Ask AI keeps aria-expanded further down, because that one really is
               a disclosure: its panel opens beside the content it belongs to.

               The group renders only when there is a takeover to switch to. With
               every takeover feature off, a lone "Workspace" option would be a
               control with nothing to choose between. *@
            @if (SurfaceSwitcherVisible)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--loose header-group--surface"
                             AriaLabel="Surface"
                             TestId="workspace-surface-switcher">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="WorkspaceVisible"
                                  id="workspace-surface-option"
                                  TestId="workspace-surface-option"
                                  aria-controls="workspace"
                                  PressedChanged="CloseSurface">
                        Workspace
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools))
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="ToolsVisible"
                                      id="tools-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="tools-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="tools-pane"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleTools">
                            Tools
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                TestId="tools-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }

                    @* One segment for two features. The Dashboard surface holds the
                       Sessions module's list as its second tab, so the segment is
                       offered while either has something to show; which tabs the
                       surface then offers is decided on the surface, below. The
                       segment points at the surface landmark rather than at either
                       pane, because which pane is in front is the tab strip's answer
                       and not this control's. Sessions used to have a segment of its
                       own here, and its feature flag now gates its tab instead. *@
                    @if (DashboardSurfaceOffered)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="DashboardVisible"
                                      id="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="dashboard-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleDashboard">
                            Dashboard
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                TestId="dashboard-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }

            @* The roadmap band, on its own. It used to be the first option of the
               panes strip, on the argument that it is one more section on screen —
               but it is a row above the panes rather than one of them, and it plays by
               none of the strip's rules: its press is a plain on-or-off that never
               closes a pane and no pane press ever closes it, it takes no modifier,
               and no capacity rule ever blocks it. Inside the strip it read as a
               fourth pane that ought to switch and Ctrl-add, and it did neither. So it
               stands loose, the shape this header gives a control that is not one of a
               set, and wears the option's look so it still reads as navigation rather
               than as a utility.

               That is also why it is not a GlobalPane. The selection holds a
               viewport-driven capacity and a "one is always on screen" invariant, both
               of which exist because the three panes compete for the same width; the
               band competes for nothing — it is a horizontal row with its own grid
               track. Folding it in would put it in TrimToCapacity's reach and let a
               narrowed window silently evict the reader's roadmap. So it keeps its own
               shell field, the same split that keeps WorkspaceSurface out of the
               selection.

               Only while the workspace is on screen, like the strip beside it, and for
               the same reason: a press on it during a takeover changed nothing the
               reader could see. *@
            @if (WorkspaceVisible && RoadmapPaneOptionVisible)
            {
                <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                              PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                              Pressed="RoadmapBandVisible"
                              id="roadmap-band-toggle"
                              TestId="roadmap-band-toggle"
                              aria-controls="roadmap-band"
                              PressedChanged="ToggleRoadmapBand">
                    Roadmap
                    <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                        Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                        TestId="roadmap-feature-status" />
                </ToggleButton>
            }

            @* Which panes are on screen below, in one strip. The shared ButtonGroup
               rather than a hand-rolled div, so its role and label come from the
               component and its test id reaches the DOM through TestId. Its options
               are aria-pressed toggles, not tabs: several panes are on screen at once,
               so this is a multi-select, and the fused shape says so.

               Only while the workspace is on screen. It used to stay up during a
               takeover, on the argument that it configured the workspace the reader
               was about to come back to — but a press on it changed nothing the
               reader could see, and a row of buttons that do nothing reads as broken
               rather than as forward planning. The pane selection still lives
               outside WorkspaceSurface and still survives the round trip; only the
               strip that shows it goes, and it comes back exactly as it was. *@
            @if (WorkspaceVisible)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--panes"
                             AriaLabel="Visible panes"
                             TestId="global-pane-multiselect">
                    @* One pane on a plain press, one more with Ctrl held — the same
                       convention as the repository scope in Region A, and for the same
                       reason: several can be on screen at once, and the option is the
                       one control the reader is already pressing. Each pane used to carry
                       a pin, railed along its option's top edge, that held it through a
                       switch; that was a second control on every option for what one
                       modifier on the option itself now says. The tooltip names the
                       modifier, because nothing else on screen does — and names it only
                       while the window fits a second pane, since a Ctrl-press in a window
                       that fits one is a switch whatever the reader held.

                       OnClick rather than PressedChanged, because the press has to reach
                       the handler with its modifiers on; the pressed state the button
                       reports is the selection's answer and not something the handler
                       needs told. The roadmap toggle above keeps PressedChanged: it
                       takes no modifier, because it is simply on or off. *@
                    @if (InboxPaneOptionVisible)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="InboxPaneVisible"
                                      id="inbox-pane-option"
                                      TestId="inbox-pane-option"
                                      aria-controls="inbox-pane"
                                      Disabled="@PaneToggleDisabled(GlobalPane.Inbox)"
                                      Title="@PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane.Inbox, "Inbox")"
                                      OnClick="args => PressPane(GlobalPane.Inbox, args)">
                            Inbox
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                                TestId="inbox-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }

                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="TasksPaneVisible"
                                  id="backlog-pane-option"
                                  TestId="backlog-pane-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  Disabled="@PaneToggleDisabled(GlobalPane.Tasks)"
                                  Title="@PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane.Tasks, "Tasks")"
                                  OnClick="args => PressPane(GlobalPane.Tasks, args)">
                        Tasks
                    </ToggleButton>

                    @if (DevbookPaneOptionVisible)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="DevbookPaneVisible"
                                      id="devbook-pane-option"
                                      TestId="devbook-pane-option"
                                      aria-controls="repository-devbook-pane"
                                      Disabled="@PaneToggleDisabled(GlobalPane.Devbook)"
                                      Title="@PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane.Devbook, "Devbook")"
                                      OnClick="args => PressPane(GlobalPane.Devbook, args)">
                            Devbook
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)"
                                                TestId="devbook-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }
        </nav>

        @* Region C — how the workspace is doing. Ambient rather than actionable,
           which is why it is the quietest thing in the row and why it no longer
           sits in the middle of the navigation. The auto margin lives here now, so
           the status and the utilities travel to the right edge together.

           The save state used to lead this region and no longer does. It was the
           one thing here that was true of the window rather than of this screen,
           and it was therefore missing from every other route — so it went to the
           band AppFooter mounts under all of them. The sync control stayed, because
           checking GitHub is a thing you do to the list in front of you. *@
        <div class="app-header__status">
            @* Monitoring is a deliberate act, not a background poll: the backlog has
               to open instantly and offline, so nothing here waits on GitHub until
               somebody asks it to.

               Only while the task list is on screen — the list is what a check
               refreshes, and the dashboard fetches from GitHub on its own. *@
            @if (TasksListOnScreen && IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration) && State.GitHubConfigured && State.HasLinkedRows)
            {
                <IconButton BaseClass="icon-link"
                            id="github-sync-button"
                            TestId="github-sync-button"
                            Disabled="@State.GitHubSyncing"
                            AriaLabel="Check GitHub for issue and pull request updates"
                            OnClick="() => State.SyncGitHubAsync()">
                    @(State.GitHubSyncing ? "…" : "⟳")
                </IconButton>
            }
        </div>

        @* Region D — the cross-cutting concerns, at the far edge and last in
           reading order: not one of them is about the work on screen.

           Ask AI is the one accented control in the whole header. It is a designed
           first-class surface rather than a utility, and holding the accent back to
           a single control is what lets it mean something instead of becoming the
           header's wallpaper — which is what the old all-gold row had made it. *@
        <div class="app-header__utilities">
            @* Offered while there is content to ask about. The panel answers from the
               task rows in view — see BuildAiContent — so with the list off screen it
               would be answering about entries the reader cannot see, and the button
               that opens it would be a button that opens nothing about this screen. *@
            @if (AiAssistantOffered)
            {
                @* A disclosure, not a pressed state: this opens a panel beside the
                   content, so it carries aria-expanded and stays AppButton rather
                   than ToggleButton. The surface switcher above is the other case —
                   a selection, not an expansion.

                   The state is written out rather than bound to the bool: Blazor
                   renders a true bool attribute as `aria-expanded=""` and omits it
                   entirely when false, and neither is a value aria-expanded accepts.
                   Now that this is the header's only disclosure, it is the one place
                   that has to say "true" or "false" — which is what the two takeover
                   buttons did before they became a selection. *@
                <AppButton BaseClass="header-accent"
                           Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                           id="ai-toggle-button"
                           TestId="ai-toggle-button"
                           aria-controls="ai-assistant-panel"
                           aria-expanded="@(_aiExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                           OnClick="ToggleAiPanel">
                    Ask AI
                    <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        TestId="ai-feature-status" />
                </AppButton>
            }

            @* Two utilities left, and they are the two that are still about this
               screen: Ask AI opens a panel beside its content, and settings is
               where this screen's own configuration lives. The version control
               and the Report issue button were neither, and they are now in the
               app-shell footer MainLayout mounts below every route. *@
            <a class="icon-link" href="settings" id="settings-link" data-testid="settings-link" title="Settings" aria-label="Settings">⚙</a>
        </div>
    </header>

    @* Gated the way its button is: the panel follows the list it reads from, and
       comes back open if the reader left it open, because _aiExpanded is not
       touched by a takeover. *@
    @if (AiAssistantOffered && _aiExpanded)
    {
        <section class="ai-panel" id="ai-assistant-panel" data-testid="ai-assistant-panel" aria-labelledby="ai-assistant-title">
            @* The library's section header. This one stays a div: the panel is
               already a section named by its heading, and the header it had was
               never a landmark of its own. *@
            <SectionHeader Element="div"
                           BaseClass="ai-panel__header"
                           TextCssClass=""
                           EyebrowCssClass="ai-panel__eyebrow"
                           TitleCssClass=""
                           ActionsCssClass=""
                           Eyebrow="Azure Foundry"
                           TitleId="ai-assistant-title"
                           Title="Ask about this content">
                <Actions>
                    <a class="ai-panel__settings" href="settings">Settings</a>
                </Actions>
            </SectionHeader>
            <p class="ai-panel__body">
                The question is answered from the entries currently in view and loaded backlog knowledge.
            </p>
            @* The box keeps its own class and its own border, so the library's
               grow wrapper is emitted without one. *@
            <TextArea Bare="true"
                      AutoGrow="false"
                      GrowCssClass=""
                      InputCssClass="ai-panel__question"
                      AriaLabel="Ask about this content"
                      data-testid="ai-question-input"
                      Rows="3"
                      Placeholder="What should I focus on next?"
                      Value="@_aiQuestion"
                      ValueChanged="value => _aiQuestion = value"
                      @onkeydown="OnAiQuestionKeyDownAsync" />
            <div class="ai-panel__actions">
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                           data-testid="ai-ask-button"
                           Disabled="@_aiBusy"
                           OnClick="AskAiAsync">
                    @(_aiBusy ? "Asking..." : "Ask")
                </AppButton>
            </div>
            @* The failure used to be a line under this box. It is a toast now: the
               panel is a takeover the reader opens over their work, and a refusal
               that stayed pinned inside it was still there next time they opened it.
               See AskAiAsync, where each catch publishes on its way out. *@
            @if (_aiAnswer is not null)
            {
                <article class="ai-panel__answer" data-testid="ai-answer">
                    <h3>Answer</h3>
                    <p>@_aiAnswer</p>
                </article>
            }
        </section>
    }

    @* Exactly one of these renders, and each one's root is the page's only
       <main>. A takeover is deliberately not a Modal: there is no scrim and no
       focus trap, because the header behind it is not inert — it is where the
       control that closes the surface lives. Modal.razor still owns the modal
       semantics for the update and feedback dialogs, which really are modal. *@
    @if (ToolsVisible)
    {
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              data-testid="tools-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              aria-label="System tools">
            <ToolsPane OnClose="CloseSurface" />
        </main>
    }
    else if (DashboardVisible)
    {
        @* Two views of one surface: the Dashboard module's overview and the
           Sessions module's list. Composed here rather than inside either pane,
           because the panes belong to two bounded contexts and neither should
           reference the other's project to draw a strip above itself — which
           surfaces exist and how they are arranged is exactly the shell's job.
           Each pane keeps its own header and its own ✕, so a tab is not a second
           place to close the surface from.

           The strip renders only when there are two tabs to choose between. Each
           tab is gated on its own feature, and with one feature off the surface
           is that feature's pane on its own — a tab strip with one tab is the
           same dead control the surface switcher refuses to be. *@
        <main class="workspace-surface"
              id="dashboard-surface"
              data-testid="dashboard-surface"
              tabindex="-1"
              @ref="_surfaceElement"
              @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
              aria-label="Dashboard">
            @if (DashboardTabsVisible)
            {
                @* Bare, so the strip and the panels sit directly in the landmark and
                   the panel — not a wrapper — is the flex child the surface's scroll
                   rule reaches. The list keeps the library's own class beside this
                   surface's, so it takes the shared tab styling and only its place
                   in the column from app.css. *@
                <Tabs Bare="true"
                      ListElement="nav"
                      ListCssClass="tabs__list workspace-surface__tabs"
                      ListTestId="dashboard-tabs"
                      AriaLabel="Dashboard views"
                      ActiveId="@DashboardTabId(ActiveDashboardTab)"
                      ActiveIdChanged="ChooseDashboardTab">
                    <TabPanel Id="@DashboardTabId(DashboardTab.Overview)"
                              Title="Overview"
                              BaseClass="workspace-surface__tabpanel"
                              TabTestId="dashboard-overview-tab"
                              TestId="dashboard-overview-tabpanel">
                        @DashboardOverview
                    </TabPanel>
                    <TabPanel Id="@DashboardTabId(DashboardTab.Sessions)"
                              Title="Sessions"
                              BaseClass="workspace-surface__tabpanel"
                              TabTestId="dashboard-sessions-tab"
                              TestId="dashboard-sessions-tabpanel">
                        <TitleContent>
                            Sessions
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                                TestId="sessions-feature-status" />
                        </TitleContent>
                        <ChildContent>
                            @SessionsList
                        </ChildContent>
                    </TabPanel>
                </Tabs>
            }
            else if (SessionsTabOffered)
            {
                @SessionsList
            }
            else
            {
                @DashboardOverview
            }
        </main>
    }
    else
    {
        <main class="@WorkspaceClass" id="workspace" data-testid="workspace">
            @if (RoadmapBandVisible)
            {
                <RoadmapBand />
            }

            @* The pane row. It was the <main> itself until the band arrived above
               it; the class, the width variable and the test id are unchanged,
               because the resizer's JavaScript finds the layout by that test id. *@
            <div class="devbook-layout @((TasksPaneVisible && RightSidePaneVisible) ? "devbook-layout--side-open" : "devbook-layout--side-closed") @(InboxBeforeTasksVisible ? "devbook-layout--inbox-before-backlog" : string.Empty)" style="--devbook-panel-width: @_devbookPanelWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)rem" data-testid="devbook-layout">
                @if (InboxBeforeTasksVisible)
                {
                    @InboxPaneContent
                }

                @if (TasksPaneVisible)
                {
                    <TasksPane />
                }

                @if (RightSidePaneVisible)
                {
                    @if (TasksPaneVisible)
                    {
                        @* The pane edge is the handle. Dragging it is the obvious gesture, so
                           the slider that used to sit inside the pane is gone; arrow keys on
                           the same separator carry the keyboard equivalent. *@
                        <div class="pane-resizer"
                             data-testid="side-pane-resizer"
                             data-pane-resizer="true"
                             role="separator"
                             aria-orientation="vertical"
                             aria-label="Side pane width"
                             aria-valuemin="@MinSidePaneWidthRem"
                             aria-valuemax="@_maxSidePaneWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                             aria-valuenow="@_devbookPanelWidthRem.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                             tabindex="0"
                             @onkeydown="OnResizerKeyDown"
                             @onkeydown:preventDefault="true">
                            <span class="pane-resizer__grip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        </div>
                    }

                    <aside @key=@("side-pane-stack") class="side-pane-stack @(TasksPaneVisible ? string.Empty : "side-pane-stack--full")" data-testid="side-pane-stack" aria-label="Tasks side panes">
                        @if (!TasksPaneVisible && InboxPaneVisible)
                        {
                            @InboxPaneContent
                        }

                        @if (DevbookPaneVisible)
                        {
                            @* The anchor and not the whole scope: the pane reads one
                               repository's folders, and the first one taken is the one
                               the reader was already reading beside. *@
                            <DevbookPane RepositoryAlias="@State.AnchorRepositoryAlias" />
                        }
                    </aside>
                }
            </div>
        </main>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private WorkspaceSurface _surface = WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;

    // Which of the Dashboard's views is in front. Kept when the surface closes,
    // for the same reason the pane selection is: coming back to the dashboard is
    // coming back to where the reader was, not to the first tab. It is written to
    // the navigation file with the surface — see WorkspaceSurfaceNames — so a
    // relaunch lands on it too.
    private DashboardTab _dashboardTab = DashboardTab.Overview;
    private bool _aiExpanded;
    private GlobalPaneSelection _globalPanes = new();
    private double _devbookPanelWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.DefaultRem;

    // Whether the roadmap band is on screen. Session-only and deliberately so:
    // WorkspaceSettingsStore scopes itself to where the backlog lives, which GitHub
    // repository backs that folder and which knowledge folders are configured —
    // three settings that are one decision, and what the reader currently has in
    // view is not that decision. The precedent for shell view state is
    // _devbookPanelWidthRem above, which is session-only for the same reason. It
    // is not a feature flag either: the flag decides whether a band exists at all,
    // this decides whether the one that exists is showing, so nothing here is reset
    // when the flag changes.
    //
    // Its own field rather than a GlobalPane, for the reason written on the
    // multiselect group in the markup above: the band has no capacity rule and must
    // not acquire one by joining a selection that has.
    //
    // Hidden by default, and left at the field default to say so. The workspace
    // opens on the panes, which are what a session is usually about; a planning row
    // above them every time is a claim that every session starts with planning, and
    // most do not. The option in the header strip is the way in, and once pressed the
    // band stays up for the rest of the session.
    private bool _roadmapVisible;
    private double _maxSidePaneWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.MaxRem;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Home>? _selfReference;
    private ElementReference _surfaceElement;
    private bool _focusSurfacePending;
    private bool _workspaceWasHidden;

    private const double MinSidePaneWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.MinRem;
    private string _aiQuestion = string.Empty;
    private string? _aiAnswer;
    private bool _aiBusy;

    /// <summary>The id the refusal carried while it was an inline alert under the
    /// question box. It rides on the toast now, so a selector written against
    /// either surface still finds the sentence.</summary>
    private const string AiErrorTestId = "ai-error";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Reopen on the surface the reader was last looking at rather than
        // always defaulting to the workspace panes. An unrecognised or absent
        // value — first launch included — leaves the field defaults in place.
        if (WorkspaceSurfaceNames.TryParse(ShellNavigation.LastSurface, out var restoredSurface, out var restoredTab))
        {
            _surface = restoredSurface;
            _dashboardTab = restoredTab;
        }

        // Same restore for which panes were open. This is what makes it survive
        // Settings and back, not only a full relaunch: the Router disposes and
        // reconstructs this component on every route change, so a fresh
        // GlobalPaneSelection is exactly what a bare `new()` field initializer
        // would otherwise hand back.
        var restoredPanes = ShellNavigation.LastEnabledPanes
            .Select(name => GlobalPaneSelection.TryParsePersistedPane(name, out var pane) ? pane : (GlobalPane?)null)
            .Where(pane => pane.HasValue)
            .Select(pane => pane!.Value)
            .ToArray();

        if (restoredPanes.Length > 0)
        {
            _globalPanes = new GlobalPaneSelection(restoredPanes);
        }

        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Routed += HandleInboxRouted;
        Features.Changed += HandleFeaturesChanged;

        // Optional, the way Settings resolves it: a host without task sync
        // composes no worker, and a test rendering this page should not have to.
        _taskSync = ResolveTaskSync();
        if (_taskSync is not null) _taskSync.Changed += HandleTaskSyncChanged;

        await State.InitializeAsync();
        SynchronizePaneAvailability();
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            _selfReference = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneResizer.initialize", _selfReference);
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Without the interop the separator still resizes by keyboard.
            }
        }

        // The Inbox loads the first time its pane is on screen rather than when
        // this page does, because the pane ships behind a flag and is closed by
        // default — a store read for a pane nobody opened is a store read wasted.
        // After every render rather than on a toggle handler, because there are
        // three ways the pane opens (its own toggle, a restored selection, and
        // the feature switching on) and one place to notice is better than three.
        if (InboxPaneVisible && !_inboxInitialized)
        {
            _inboxInitialized = true;
            await InboxState.InitializeAsync();
        }

        // The two branches below run on every render, not only the first: a
        // surface can open and close any number of times after it.
        if (WorkspaceVisible)
        {
            _focusSurfacePending = false;

            if (_workspaceWasHidden)
            {
                _workspaceWasHidden = false;

                // A takeover unmounts the pane layout, and the bounds are measured
                // from that element, so the window's resize listener has nothing to
                // report for as long as one is open. Without this, a window resized
                // during a takeover would leave the pane capacity describing the
                // window as it was before — until the next resize happened to
                // correct it. Re-measuring here applies the capacity rule for the
                // window the reader actually came back to.
                try
                {
                    await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneResizer.refresh");
                }
                catch (Exception)
                {
                    // Non-fatal for the same reason initialize is: without the
                    // interop the capacity simply stays where it was.
                }
            }

            return;
        }

        _workspaceWasHidden = true;

        // Moving focus onto the surface is what announces the context change and
        // makes Escape work without a click first. Once per open — guarded on the
        // flag rather than on firstRender, because a re-render inside an open
        // surface must not drag focus back out of whatever it is now on.
        if (!_focusSurfacePending) return;

        _focusSurfacePending = false;
        try
        {
            await _surfaceElement.FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Focus is an improvement on where the browser would have left it,
            // not something the surface depends on.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the tools surface is on screen. Unlike the three global
    /// panes this is a plain disclosure with no capacity rule — it takes the whole
    /// screen instead of a place in the side stack, so nothing else renders beside
    /// it — but it is still gated on the feature, so turning the feature off
    /// returns the reader to the workspace.</summary>
    private bool ToolsVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.Tools && IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools);

    /// <summary>Whether the dashboard is on screen. The same shape as
    /// <see cref="ToolsVisible"/>, and mutually exclusive with it by construction
    /// rather than by a rule written here: both read the one surface field.</summary>
    private bool DashboardVisible => _surface == WorkspaceSurface.Dashboard && DashboardSurfaceOffered;

    /// <summary>Whether the Dashboard surface has anything to show. Two features
    /// share the surface — the overview is the Dashboard's and the session list is
    /// Sessions' — so the surface exists while either does, and each tab answers
    /// for itself below. The flag Sessions reads is its own, not the shell's,
    /// because the pane is that context's too.</summary>
    private bool DashboardSurfaceOffered => OverviewTabOffered || SessionsTabOffered;

    private bool OverviewTabOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard);

    private bool SessionsTabOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(SessionFeatures.Sessions);

    /// <summary>Whether there is a strip to draw: two tabs, or no strip.</summary>
    private bool DashboardTabsVisible => OverviewTabOffered && SessionsTabOffered;

    /// <summary>The tab in front, with the feature read first: a remembered
    /// Sessions tab whose feature has since gone off shows the overview, and keeps
    /// the field as it was for when the feature comes back.</summary>
    private DashboardTab ActiveDashboardTab =>
        _dashboardTab == DashboardTab.Sessions && !SessionsTabOffered ? DashboardTab.Overview : _dashboardTab;

    /// <summary>Whether the panes are on screen. Anything that is not a takeover
    /// is the workspace, including a surface whose feature has been switched off.</summary>
    private bool WorkspaceVisible => !ToolsVisible && !DashboardVisible;

    /// <summary>Whether the task list is in front of the reader — on screen in the
    /// workspace, not merely selected underneath a takeover. The GitHub check and
    /// Ask AI both act on the list, and both are offered on this answer.</summary>
    private bool TasksListOnScreen => WorkspaceVisible && TasksPaneVisible;

    /// <summary>Whether what is on screen can show several repositories at once:
    /// the task list, or the Dashboard — both of its tabs take the whole scope.
    /// Devbook reads one, and Tools reads none, so on those a chip is a single
    /// select and the scope is kept to its anchor.</summary>
    private bool RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral => TasksListOnScreen || DashboardVisible;

    private bool AiAssistantOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatures.AiAssistant) && TasksListOnScreen;

    /// <summary>Whether the surface switcher is offered at all. It needs something
    /// to switch to: with every takeover feature off its only member would be the
    /// workspace the reader is already looking at, which is a control with no choice
    /// in it. Each takeover keeps its own segment gated on its own feature, so this
    /// is the "is there a segment beyond Workspace" question and nothing more.</summary>
    private bool SurfaceSwitcherVisible =>
        IsFeatureEnabled(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools) || DashboardSurfaceOffered;

    /// <summary>Whether the Roadmap option is offered at all. The same two-level
    /// split as <see cref="InboxPaneOptionVisible"/>: the feature decides whether
    /// there is an option, the option decides what is on screen.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapPaneOptionVisible => IsFeatureEnabled(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap);

    /// <summary>Whether the roadmap band renders above the panes. Only inside the
    /// workspace: a takeover hides every other domain concern, the band included.
    /// The flag is read first, so turning the feature off hides the band whatever
    /// the toggle says — and leaves the toggle's own state untouched for when it
    /// comes back.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapBandVisible => RoadmapPaneOptionVisible && _roadmapVisible;

    /// <summary>The workspace grid's classes. One modifier, because show and hide is
    /// binary: with no band on screen its track goes rather than being left empty
    /// above the panes, and there is no in-between size for a second variant to
    /// describe. The feature being off and the toggle being off are the same
    /// layout.</summary>
    private string WorkspaceClass => RoadmapBandVisible ? "workspace" : "workspace workspace--no-roadmap";

    private void ToggleAiPanel() => _aiExpanded = !_aiExpanded;

    private void ToggleTools() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.Tools);

    private void ToggleDashboard() => ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface.Dashboard);

    /// <summary>Opens a surface, or returns to the workspace if it was already the
    /// one on screen. Assigning rather than flipping a flag is what makes the
    /// takeovers exclusive: opening one is the same act as closing the other.</summary>
    private void ToggleSurface(WorkspaceSurface surface)
    {
        _surface = _surface == surface ? WorkspaceSurface.Workspace : surface;
        _focusSurfacePending = _surface != WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>Back to the panes. The pane selection was never touched, so there
    /// is nothing to restore — it is simply visible again.</summary>
    private void CloseSurface()
    {
        _surface = WorkspaceSurface.Workspace;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>The strip's press. The id is the enum name in lower case, which is
    /// what <see cref="DashboardTabId"/> hands the strip; an id this build does not
    /// know is ignored rather than guessed at.</summary>
    private void ChooseDashboardTab(string id)
    {
        if (!Enum.TryParse<DashboardTab>(id, ignoreCase: true, out var tab) || !Enum.IsDefined(tab)) return;

        _dashboardTab = tab;
        RememberSurface();
    }

    private static string DashboardTabId(DashboardTab tab) => tab.ToString().ToLowerInvariant();

    /// <summary>What the reader is looking at, written for the next fresh shell
    /// instance. The tab travels with the surface, so a reader on the sessions
    /// list reopens on the sessions list.</summary>
    private void RememberSurface() =>
        ShellNavigation.SetLastSurface(WorkspaceSurfaceNames.Name(_surface, ActiveDashboardTab));

    /// <summary>The two Dashboard views, each written once. The surface renders one
    /// of them inside a tab or on its own depending on how many are offered, and a
    /// pane written twice in the markup would be two places to keep in step.</summary>
    private RenderFragment DashboardOverview =>
        @<DashboardPane OnClose="CloseSurface" RepositoryAliases="@State.SelectedRepositoryAliases" />;

    private RenderFragment SessionsList =>
        @<SessionsPane OnClose="CloseSurface"
                       RepositoryScope="@State.SelectedRepositoryAliases"
                       RepositoryAlias="@RepositoryAliasFor"
                       RepositoryColour="@RepositoryColourFor"
                       OnOpenTask="OpenPlanEntryAsync" />;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Backlog entry a delivery run was started from, opened from the sessions
    /// list: back to the panes, the task list on screen, that entry selected.
    /// <para>
    /// Here rather than in the sessions pane because an entry belongs to Tasks and
    /// that pane holds sessions: it knows a run names an entry and nothing about how
    /// this workspace shows one. The shell owns both, which is what makes it the one
    /// place the two can meet.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Matched on the pair the import writes down — the plan and the item's id within
    /// it — because that is what identifies an entry across plan versions; the plan is
    /// stored with its sigil and the marker states the slug, so the comparison drops
    /// it. An entry this workspace does not hold is said out loud rather than
    /// silently doing nothing: the run was real and the entry is somewhere, just not
    /// here.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OpenPlanEntryAsync(DeliveryRunReference reference)
    {
        var row = State.Rows.FirstOrDefault(candidate =>
            string.Equals(candidate.PreviewImportItemId, reference.Label, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            && InPlan(candidate, reference.Plan));

        if (row is null)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                $"No entry of plan {reference.Plan} with the id {reference.Label} is in this workspace.",
                PlanEntryMissingTestId));

            return;
        }

        CloseSurface();
        _globalPanes.TrySetEnabled(GlobalPane.Tasks, enabled: true);
        PersistPaneSelection();

        await State.SelectAsync(row);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether an entry belongs to the plan a run names. Two ways, because an entry
    /// can carry the plan in two: Import stamps the plan id on what it writes, and an
    /// entry typed by hand carries the plan tag and nothing else. Either is the plan
    /// saying so; neither is a guess.
    /// <para>
    /// The sigil is trimmed on both sides — the app stores a plan tag as
    /// <c>+slug</c> and the marker states the slug bare — and a reference that names
    /// no plan matches on the item id alone, which the caller has already compared.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static bool InPlan(EntryRow row, string? plan)
    {
        if (plan is null) return true;

        var wanted = plan.TrimStart('+');

        return string.Equals((row.ImportPlanId ?? string.Empty).TrimStart('+'), wanted, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            || row.PreviewTags.Any(tag => string.Equals(tag.TrimStart('+'), wanted, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
    }

    private const string PlanEntryMissingTestId = "sessions-plan-entry-missing";

    private void OnSurfaceKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") CloseSurface();
    }

    private async Task AskAiAsync()
    {
        if (_aiBusy) return;

        _aiBusy = true;
        _aiAnswer = null;

        try
        {
            var response = await AzureFoundryChat.AskAsync(new AzureFoundryChatRequest(BuildAiContent(), _aiQuestion));
            _aiAnswer = response.Answer;
        }
        catch (AzureFoundryException ex)
        {
            // The one exception the client throws for every way an answer fails
            // to be one: a bad status, an endpoint that could not be reached, and
            // a request that ran out of time - the pipeline's timeout included,
            // which used to arrive here as Polly's own exception, past the two
            // transport catches this handler kept, and stop the whole page.
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error(ex.Message, AiErrorTestId));
        }
        finally
        {
            _aiBusy = false;
        }
    }

    private Task OnAiQuestionKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e) =>
        e.Key == "Enter" && (e.CtrlKey || e.MetaKey) ? AskAiAsync() : Task.CompletedTask;

    private string BuildAiContent()
    {
        var builder = new System.Text.StringBuilder();
        builder.AppendLine("Visible tasks:");
        foreach (var row in State.FilteredRows.Where(row => !row.IsUntouched))
        {
            builder.AppendLine("---");
            builder.AppendLine(row.RawText.Trim());
        }

        var content = builder.ToString().Trim();
        return content.Length <= 12000 ? content : content[..12000];
    }

    private bool TasksPaneVisible => _globalPanes.IsEnabled(GlobalPane.Tasks);

    private bool InboxFeatureEnabled => IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatures.InboxPane);

    private bool InboxPaneOptionVisible => InboxFeatureEnabled;

    private bool InboxPaneVisible => InboxPaneOptionVisible && _globalPanes.IsEnabled(GlobalPane.Inbox);

    /// <summary>Whether there is any knowledge to offer. The shell asks Devbook
    /// rather than working it out from folder settings itself.</summary>
    private bool DevbookPaneOptionVisible =>
        IsFeatureEnabled(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)
        && Devbook.VisibleAreas(State.AnchorRepositoryAlias).Count > 0;

    private bool DevbookPaneVisible => DevbookPaneOptionVisible && _globalPanes.IsEnabled(GlobalPane.Devbook);

    private bool InboxBeforeTasksVisible => TasksPaneVisible && InboxPaneVisible;

    /// <summary>Whether the side stack has anything in it. Tools used to count
    /// here; it no longer lives in the stack at all, so a stack holding only the
    /// tools pane can no longer happen and the split cannot open for it.</summary>
    private bool RightSidePaneVisible => DevbookPaneVisible || (InboxPaneVisible && !TasksPaneVisible);

    private bool IsFeatureEnabled(string featureKey) => Features.IsEnabled(featureKey);

    /// <summary>The maturity flag an entry point wears — empty unless the feature
    /// behind it is both switched on and unfinished.
    ///
    /// <para>The settings screen shows a status whether or not the switch is on,
    /// because it is describing the switch. Here the badge is describing
    /// something you can reach and click, so a feature that is off has nothing to
    /// flag. Both halves of that live in <see cref="AppFeatureStatusBadge"/>
    /// rather than being spelled out at each of the entry points below.</para></summary>
    private string FeatureStatusSlug(string featureKey) =>
        AppFeatureStatusBadge.SlugFor(AppFeatures.All, Features, featureKey);

    private string? FeatureStatusTitle(string featureKey) =>
        AppFeatureStatusBadge.TitleFor(AppFeatures.All, Features, featureKey);

    private void HandleStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        SynchronizePaneAvailability();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    private void HandleFeaturesChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        SynchronizePaneAvailability();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>Whether the Inbox has been told to load. Once per page: the
    /// state itself outlives the page and keeps what it loaded, so a second
    /// Home — Settings and back — asks again and gets a fresh read, which is
    /// the cheap and correct answer for a store another device may have
    /// written to meanwhile.</summary>
    private bool _inboxInitialized;

    /// <summary>The replication loop, or null in a host that composes none. The
    /// Inbox listens to it here rather than itself because the Inbox's UI may
    /// not see the sync adapter — the shell may, and forwarding one event is
    /// the whole of what it does with it.</summary>
    private TaskSyncWorker? _taskSync;

    private TaskSyncWorker? ResolveTaskSync()
    {
        try
        {
            return Services.GetService<TaskSyncWorker>();
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            // Registered but not constructible here — the same case Settings
            // reads as "this host does not replicate".
            return null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A pull may have delivered a capture or another device's edits.
    /// Reloading either pane is a database read, so the Inbox is re-read only
    /// once it has loaded at all — before that the first load will read
    /// whatever arrived — and the Tasks pane only when a cycle actually
    /// applied something it had not applied before. The worker raises this at
    /// both ends of a cycle and for cycles that changed nothing, and a list
    /// that started over on every one of those would replace its rows every
    /// five minutes for no reader's benefit.
    /// <para>
    /// The Tasks reload is what the timestamp poll used to do — a pull writes
    /// the same database the poll watched — and the state keeps that poll's
    /// guard: a reload that arrives under a live caret is owed rather than
    /// dropped, and lands when the caret goes.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The task is not awaited — the event is synchronous and the renderer's
    /// dispatcher owns the continuation — so a failure inside it has nowhere
    /// to go but the toast. The catch is on this side of <c>InvokeAsync</c> so
    /// an unobserved exception cannot take the circuit down for a read the
    /// reader never asked for.
    /// </para></summary>
    private void HandleTaskSyncChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        if (_taskSync is { IsSyncing: false, LastSummary: { Applied: > 0 } summary } && summary.At != _lastAppliedSyncAt)
        {
            _lastAppliedSyncAt = summary.At;

            try
            {
                await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after a sync: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
            }
        }

        if (!_inboxInitialized) return;

        try
        {
            await InboxState.ReloadAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The Inbox could not be refreshed after a sync: {ex.Message}", InboxReloadErrorTestId));
        }
    });

    /// <summary>The cycle whose applied changes the Tasks pane last reloaded
    /// for, so the raise at the end of that same cycle — and every raise for
    /// a cycle that applied nothing since — reloads it no second time.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? _lastAppliedSyncAt;

    /// <summary>Where the two discarded reloads report a failure, for a test
    /// to find and a reader to read.</summary>
    private const string InboxReloadErrorTestId = "inbox-reload-error";
    private const string TasksReloadErrorTestId = "tasks-reload-error";

    /// <summary>Whether a capture run is in flight, and what the last one said.
    /// Held here rather than in the pane for the reason the Inbox's own view
    /// state lives in <see cref="InboxDesktopState"/>: the pane re-mounts when
    /// it changes slot, and a run's result dropping at that moment would leave
    /// the reader with a button that did nothing. The Inbox's Add is not
    /// answered here at all — the pane files it through its own module.</summary>
    private bool _captureRunning;
    private string? _captureMessage;

    /// <summary>Whether the sources panel on the Inbox pane is open. Held
    /// here for the same reason the run's result is: the pane re-mounts when
    /// it changes slot, and a panel that snapped shut on that would be one the
    /// reader has to keep reopening.</summary>
    private bool _captureSourcesOpen;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Inbox pane as this shell composes it, once, for both slots it can
    /// sit in: the Capture button answered by <see cref="RunCaptureAsync"/>,
    /// and the Capture context's own sources panel in the pane's sources slot.
    /// The pane draws that slot without naming what is in it, so the Inbox
    /// stays free of Capture's types while its button and its switches sit
    /// together on screen.
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment InboxPaneContent =>
        @<InboxPane OnCapture="RunCaptureAsync" CaptureRunning="_captureRunning" CaptureMessage="@_captureMessage">
            <Sources>
                <CaptureSourcesPanel Expanded="_captureSourcesOpen" ExpandedChanged="open => _captureSourcesOpen = open" />
            </Sources>
        </InboxPane>;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Inbox's Capture, answered: one run over the sources switched on in
    /// Settings, reported back as a sentence the pane can print — and, when the
    /// run delivered anything, the Inbox re-read so the new rows are on screen
    /// without a restart, the way a sync pull re-reads it.
    /// <para>
    /// The runner never throws for a source — each answers for itself in the
    /// result — so a throw here is the run itself failing to start, and that is
    /// reported in the same line rather than left to the console. The re-read
    /// is kept out of that try: its failure is the Inbox's to report, not the
    /// run's, and the run's own line stands.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task RunCaptureAsync()
    {
        if (_captureRunning) return;

        _captureRunning = true;
        _captureMessage = null;
        CaptureRunResultDto? run = null;

        try
        {
            run = await CaptureRunner.RunAsync();
            _captureMessage = Describe(run);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _captureMessage = $"Capture could not run: {ex.Message}";
        }
        finally
        {
            _captureRunning = false;
        }

        if (run is { TotalNewItems: > 0 }) await ReloadInboxAfterCaptureAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The same read <see cref="HandleTaskSyncChanged"/> does after a
    /// pull, for the same reason and with the same guard: before the pane has
    /// loaded at all, the first load will read whatever the run delivered.</summary>
    private async Task ReloadInboxAfterCaptureAsync()
    {
        if (!_inboxInitialized) return;

        try
        {
            await InboxState.ReloadAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The Inbox could not be refreshed after a capture: {ex.Message}", InboxReloadErrorTestId));
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A run's result in one line. Nothing enabled points at the
    /// panel that would change that — it is on the same pane, under this line;
    /// otherwise each source's own sentence — which already carries that
    /// source's count — and, when there was more than one source, the total,
    /// so a reader is not left adding up.</summary>
    private static string Describe(CaptureRunResultDto run)
    {
        if (run.NothingEnabled)
        {
            return "No capture sources are enabled. Open Sources below and switch one on.";
        }

        var sources = string.Join(" ", run.Sources.Select(source => source.Message));

        if (run.Sources.Count == 1) return sources;

        var total = run.TotalNewItems == 1 ? "1 new item in all." : $"{run.TotalNewItems} new items in all.";

        return $"{sources} {total}";
    }

    private void SynchronizePaneAvailability()
    {
        _globalPanes.TrySetAvailable(GlobalPane.Tasks, available: true);
        _globalPanes.TrySetAvailable(GlobalPane.Inbox, InboxPaneOptionVisible);
        _globalPanes.TrySetAvailable(GlobalPane.Devbook, DevbookPaneOptionVisible);
        PersistPaneSelection();
    }

    /// <summary>Remembers which panes are open, so the next fresh shell
    /// instance — a relaunch, or the Router rebuilding this component after a
    /// trip to Settings — reopens on them instead of the single default
    /// pane.</summary>
    private void PersistPaneSelection()
    {
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();

        var enabled = Enum.GetValues<GlobalPane>().Where(_globalPanes.IsEnabled).Select(pane => pane.ToString()).ToArray();
        ShellNavigation.SetLastPanes(enabled);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The scope holds several repositories only while something on screen can show
    /// several — see <see cref="RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral"/>.
    /// <para>
    /// Several at once is a list affordance: the backlog list and the dashboard can
    /// show more than one repository, and the Devbook pane reads exactly one. So
    /// when the list goes — closed by the reader, trimmed by a window that no longer
    /// fits it, or left behind for a surface that reads one — the scope drops back
    /// to the anchor, and what the Devbook pane was reading is what it keeps
    /// reading. Called from every path that changes what is on screen: the pane
    /// toggles and the restore go through <see cref="PersistPaneSelection"/>, the
    /// surface switches call it themselves, and so does the capacity trim, because
    /// a trim is deliberately not persisted.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits()
    {
        if (!RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral) State.NarrowRepositoryScopeToAnchor();
    }

    private bool PaneToggleDisabled(GlobalPane pane)
    {
        if (_globalPanes.IsEnabled(pane))
        {
            return !_globalPanes.CanDisable(pane);
        }

        return !_globalPanes.CanEnable(pane);
    }

    /// <summary>Show or hide the band. A plain flip of a shell field rather than a
    /// call into <see cref="GlobalPaneSelection"/>, and it never consults capacity:
    /// the band is always freely toggleable because it takes no width from the panes.
    /// See the roadmap toggle in the markup for why it stays out of the selection.</summary>
    private void ToggleRoadmapBand() => _roadmapVisible = !_roadmapVisible;

    /// <summary>A press on a pane's option, modifiers and all. A closed pane opens
    /// on its own with a plain press and beside the open ones with Ctrl (Cmd on
    /// macOS) held; an open pane closes either way, because "this one too" has no
    /// meaning for a pane already on screen. Same shape as
    /// <see cref="ScopeRepository"/>, which is the convention the reader learns from
    /// the chips beside this strip.</summary>
    private void PressPane(GlobalPane pane, MouseEventArgs args)
    {
        if (!_globalPanes.IsEnabled(pane) && (args.CtrlKey || args.MetaKey) && _globalPanes.TakesSeveral)
        {
            _globalPanes.TryOpenBeside(pane);
        }
        else
        {
            _globalPanes.Toggle(pane);
        }

        PersistPaneSelection();
    }

    /// <summary>What the press will do, and the modifier that changes it — named
    /// here because nothing else on screen does. A window that fits one pane names
    /// none: Ctrl would only switch there, and a tooltip offering a modifier that
    /// does nothing is a control lying about itself.</summary>
    private string PaneOptionTitle(GlobalPane pane, string name)
    {
        if (_globalPanes.IsEnabled(pane))
        {
            return _globalPanes.CanDisable(pane) ? $"Close {name}" : $"{name} is the only pane open";
        }

        return _globalPanes.TakesSeveral
            ? $"Switch to {name} — Ctrl+click to open it beside the open panes"
            : $"Switch to {name}";
    }

    /// <summary>Triage: the Inbox turned an item into backlog entries, and the
    /// shell shows them where work actually happens.
    /// <para>
    /// Alongside rather than a switch: this is the shell opening a pane on the
    /// reader's behalf, and closing the Inbox they just routed the item from
    /// would take away the queue they were working through.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The Tasks pane is reloaded from the store rather than told what changed.
    /// The Inbox's module wrote the entries through a port an adapter answers,
    /// so as far as the Tasks state is concerned somebody else wrote its
    /// database — which is exactly the case its store reload already handles.
    /// The shell knows both panes exist and nothing about what either holds.
    /// </para></summary>
    private void HandleInboxRouted(InboxRoutedDto routed) => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        _globalPanes.TryOpenAlongside(GlobalPane.Tasks);
        PersistPaneSelection();

        try
        {
            await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // The entries were made — the Inbox said so — and only the pane
            // beside it failed to show them. Said on a toast, for the reason
            // HandleTaskSyncChanged gives: nothing awaits this.
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after routing: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// The repository's identity mark, as classes.
    /// <para>
    /// Which hue is the settings store's answer and not this screen's — every surface
    /// asks the same place, which is what stops the same project being two colours.
    /// The mark is additive: the chip keeps the surface, border and text it already
    /// had, and gains a rule down its leading edge. See
    /// <c>.design/color-scheme.md#band-identity-tokens</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? RepositoryMarkClass(string alias) =>
        State.RepositoryColours.TryGetValue(alias, out var colour) ? $"repo-mark repo-mark--{colour}" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// The hue for a repository named the way a session names one — <c>owner/name</c>,
    /// or nothing at all. Null for a session whose repository is not configured here,
    /// which is the ordinary case for work done in a clone this workspace does not
    /// know about.
    /// </summary>
    private int? RepositoryColourFor(string? repository) => State.RepositoryColourFor(repository);

    /// <summary>
    /// The alias a session's recorded repository goes by here — the sessions list
    /// matches the header's scope through this, and the scope is aliases. Null for a
    /// repository this workspace has not configured, and for a session that recorded
    /// none; the pane treats both as out of any scope.
    /// </summary>
    private string? RepositoryAliasFor(string? repository) => State.RepositoryAliasFor(repository);

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows or hides the repository identity hues across every surface.
    /// <para>
    /// The state class writes it, because the settings store is where the hue itself is
    /// answered and this is the same fact seen from the other side. The store's message
    /// on a failed write is dropped rather than shown: the choice has taken effect for
    /// this session either way, and the header has no room for a line of prose that
    /// would outlive the gesture it belongs to. Settings is where a settings file that
    /// cannot be written gets reported.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ShowRepositoryColours(bool visible) => _ = State.SetRepositoryColoursVisible(visible);

    /// <summary>
    /// A press on a scope chip.
    /// <para>
    /// The repository scope is global: it narrows the backlog list and, through the
    /// anchor handed to the pane, what knowledge is read beside it. A plain press is
    /// the single select the strip always had — this repository, or none when it was
    /// already alone in the scope. With Ctrl held (Cmd on a Mac) the press adds the
    /// repository beside the ones already scoped, or takes it back out: the
    /// convention every file manager has, so it needs no teaching beyond the tooltip.
    /// A plain press on a chip inside a wider scope narrows to that one, for the
    /// same reason — that is what a plain click on one of several selected files does.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The modifier counts only while what is on screen can show several — the task
    /// list, or the Dashboard; see <see cref="RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral"/>. Without
    /// one of those there is nothing a second repository could be shown in, so the
    /// press is an ordinary one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ScopeRepository(string repositoryAlias, MouseEventArgs args)
    {
        if ((args.CtrlKey || args.MetaKey) && RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral)
        {
            State.ToggleRepositoryInScope(repositoryAlias);
            return;
        }

        var alone = State.SelectedRepositoryAliases is [var only]
            && string.Equals(only, repositoryAlias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
        State.SetRepositoryFilter(alone ? string.Empty : repositoryAlias);
    }

    /// <summary>The identity mark, plus the anchor's own class while there is a
    /// second chip for it to be distinct from.</summary>
    private string? RepositoryScopeChipClass(string alias, bool anchor)
    {
        var mark = RepositoryMarkClass(alias);
        if (!anchor) return mark;

        return mark is null ? "chip--anchor" : mark + " chip--anchor";
    }

    /// <summary>What the chip does next, since the modifier is the one thing about
    /// the strip nothing on screen shows.</summary>
    private string RepositoryScopeChipTitle(GitHubRepositoryRef repository, bool selected, bool anchor)
    {
        // Nothing to show several in, no modifier to name: the chip says what a
        // single select says.
        if (!RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral) return selected ? $"Deselect {repository.FullName}" : repository.FullName;

        if (!selected) return $"{repository.FullName}. Ctrl+click to add it to the scope";
        if (State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count == 1) return $"Deselect {repository.FullName}";

        var name = anchor ? $"{repository.FullName} — Devbook follows this one" : repository.FullName;
        return $"{name}. Click to scope to it alone, Ctrl+click to remove it from the scope";
    }

    [JSInvokable]
    public Task SetSidePaneWidthAsync(double widthRem)
    {
        var clamped = SidePaneWidth.Clamp(widthRem, _maxSidePaneWidthRem);
        if (Math.Abs(clamped - _devbookPanelWidthRem) < 0.001) return Task.CompletedTask;

        _devbookPanelWidthRem = clamped;
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // How wide the pane may actually get depends on the window, which only the
    // browser knows; without it the separator would keep counting past the edge.
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task SetSidePaneMaxWidthAsync(double maxRem)
    {
        var clamped = SidePaneWidth.Clamp(maxRem, SidePaneWidth.MaxRem);
        var width = Math.Min(_devbookPanelWidthRem, clamped);
        if (Math.Abs(clamped - _maxSidePaneWidthRem) < 0.001 && Math.Abs(width - _devbookPanelWidthRem) < 0.001)
        {
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        _maxSidePaneWidthRem = clamped;
        _devbookPanelWidthRem = width;
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [JSInvokable]
    public Task SetGlobalPaneCapacityAsync(int capacity)
    {
        if (!_globalPanes.TrySetCapacity(capacity))
        {
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // The drag lives in JavaScript because pointer capture does; the keyboard
    // equivalent stays here so the separator has one clamped source of truth.
    private void OnResizerKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e) =>
        _devbookPanelWidthRem = SidePaneWidth.Adjust(_devbookPanelWidthRem, e.Key, _maxSidePaneWidthRem);

    // Asynchronous, because leaving the shell has an interop call to make on the
    // way out. The resizer holds a map of owners and invokes every one of them on
    // each window resize, so a Home that registered and never unregistered is a
    // dead reference the next resize calls into — the page still on screen throws
    // on a window drag because a page that is gone is still in the map.
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        State.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Routed -= HandleInboxRouted;
        Features.Changed -= HandleFeaturesChanged;
        if (_taskSync is not null) _taskSync.Changed -= HandleTaskSyncChanged;

        // Unregistered before the reference is disposed, so the map can never hold
        // one that is already dead. The other order leaves a window between the two
        // in which a resize finds a registration it cannot invoke.
        if (_selfReference is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                // No key, matching the keyless initialize above: the resizer reads
                // an omitted key as the empty slot, which is the app's own layout —
                // it carries no data-pane-owner because it predates the key. A key
                // invented here would delete nothing and leave this owner in place.
                //
                // The empty slot is shared with any future Home, so this would take
                // a newer instance's registration out if disposal of the old one ran
                // after the new one registered. The router disposes the outgoing page
                // before rendering the incoming one, so it does not — stated here so
                // that a change to that ordering is read as breaking this.
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneResizer.dispose");
            }
            catch (JSException)
            {
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
            }
            catch (InvalidOperationException)
            {
                // Disposed from a prerender: the resizer was never initialized,
                // so there is nothing to dispose.
            }
        }

        _selfReference?.Dispose();
        _selfReference = null;
    }
}
